Add a trusted-LAN raw firmware HTTP endpoint on top of the shared
OTA transaction core from patch 0015. Applied only to nanoc6-wifi
and atoms3-lite-wifi via variants.json; nanoc6-thread never
applies, compiles, links, or exposes any of the code here.

Scope, kept minimal (no page UI, no multipart parser, no Arduino
runtime, no filesystem, no UDP or TCP espota server, no password
work, no settings or log changes, no new runtime dependency, no
partition change):

  * MODIFY main/aliro_local_web.cpp:
      - Register one new route POST /api/ota against the existing
        native esp_http_server. Raise cfg.max_uri_handlers from 7
        to 8 (exactly one, to fit the new route).
      - New static handler ota_post_handler:
          * Requires Content-Type application/octet-stream, with
            an optional trailing OWS-then-';' parameter suffix,
            else 415 unsupported_content_type. Empty / missing /
            other media types are refused.
          * Requires a nonzero Content-Length, else 400
            empty_body. Uses req->content_len as size_t; never
            narrows.
          * Calls AliroLocalOtaBegin(declared) and maps the OTA
            core's error codes to a clear HTTP code:
              busy (INVALID_STATE) -> 409 busy
              invalid declared size (INVALID_ARG) -> 400 invalid_size
              declared > partition (INVALID_SIZE) -> 413 over_partition
              no OTA partition (NOT_FOUND) -> 500 no_ota_partition
              other Begin error -> 500 begin_failed
            On Begin failure the OTA core has already released the
            busy guard, so the handler does NOT call
            AliroLocalOtaAbort.
          * Streams the raw body into AliroLocalOtaWrite through
            a fixed 4 KiB stack buffer (static_assert exactly
            4096 bytes). Bounded recv retries capped at 8 so a
            stalled peer cannot loop the httpd task. Successful
            reads reset the retry counter.
          * AliroLocalOtaAbort ownership in this handler:
              - HTTP-layer failure while the handler still owns
                an open core transaction (early EOF n == 0,
                timeout exhaustion, other recv/socket error, or
                the post-loop short-body invariant) -> the
                handler calls AliroLocalOtaAbort EXACTLY ONCE
                before returning.
              - Core-method failure (AliroLocalOtaWrite or
                AliroLocalOtaFinish returns != ESP_OK) -> the
                handler does NOT call AliroLocalOtaAbort. Those
                core methods already consume the transaction and
                release the shared busy guard; a second Abort
                could race with a fresh Begin from another caller
                and abort the new owner's handle.
          * httpd_req_recv returning 0 before Content-Length is
            reached (peer closed the connection cleanly early)
            is mapped to 400 short_body, distinct from generic
            recv_failed.
          * Requires the exact advertised byte count. Post-loop
            defensive check (total_read == declared) also aborts
            + 400 short_body if the invariant is ever violated.
          * Calls AliroLocalOtaFinish and maps errors WITHOUT a
            defensive re-Abort:
              INVALID_SIZE -> 400 size_mismatch
              INVALID_STATE -> 400 invalid_state
              other -> 500 finish_failed
          * On success: sends a small no-store JSON body
            ({"ok":true,"restarting":true}). ONLY if
            httpd_resp_send returns ESP_OK does the handler wait
            with vTaskDelay(pdMS_TO_TICKS(200)) so the response
            flushes, then call esp_restart(). If the success
            response send itself failed, the handler returns
            that error and does NOT call vTaskDelay or
            esp_restart, so the peer never sees a silent reboot
            after a failed response. Failure paths NEVER call
            esp_restart.
      - Add the httpd_uri_t entry kOta and register it alongside
        the other seven routes. The trusted-LAN posture (no
        authentication) is preserved as requested.

  * WIRE the new patch into nanoc6-wifi and atoms3-lite-wifi
    source_patches in firmware/variants.json.
    nanoc6-thread source_patches is unchanged.

Explicit non-goals for this patch: no page UI, no multipart
parser, no Arduino runtime, no filesystem, no UDP / TCP espota
server, no password work, no settings or log changes, no new
runtime dependency, no partition change, no CMakeLists.txt
change (SRC_DIRS "." already picks up main/ .cpp files).

